  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nForecast is beginning to come into much greater certainty with\nthis run of the forecast models, especially in regards to a cold\nfront moving through eastern NC on Saturday and then continuing\noffshore. There is now HIGH confidence that a nor'easter will \nform offshore and bring impacts to eastern NC in the form of \nstrong winds, high waves, and coastal flooding as well as \nextremely dangerous marine conditions through the weekend.\n\nUncertainty remains on the back end of the forecast. Exactly how\nlong it lingers offshore and its eventual route upon exit still\nhave low confidence at this time. What we do know is that the\nalternate scenario that excluded the formation of the low is now\ngone and as a result the expected values for winds and seas has\nincreased.\n\nAs far as the cold front is concerned that will be the catalyst\nfor the eventual system, expect winds to shift to northeasterly\nearly Saturday morning bringing rain chances to the area for \nmuch of the day on Saturday. While amounts are not expected to \nbe extreme, a widespread half of an inch to an inch of rain \nthroughout the day is likely. \n\nWinds will pick up throughout the day with 25-30 mph gusts \ncommon. Values will increase from west to east with areas near \nthe coast experiencing much higher values. A wind advisory in\neffect for Western Carteret, Mainland Dare and Mainland Hyde \nCounties from 18z Saturday through 00z Tuesday. For the Core and\nOuter Banks as well as Downeast Carteret County, a high wind\nwatch is in effect from 00z Sunday through 6z on Monday.\n\n2-4 foot above ground level coastal flooding will be possible\nwith minor to moderate impacts throughout the Outer Banks, Core\nBanks and Downeast Carteret County. Impacts are expected to both\nsound and oceanside locales with potentially significant impacts\nto vulnerable areas on the ocean side. A mix of coastal flood\nheadlines is in effect to cover these hazards. Additionally, a \nhigh surf advisory is in effect for the Outer Banks with \nbreaking waves of generally 8-12 feet expected in the surf zone.\nA moderate to high risk of rip currents is also expected \nthrough much of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter record breaking heat the last several days, temperatures\nare expected to cool significantly. Maximum temperatures through\nthe period are expected to remain in the 60-70 degree range with\ncooler values inland and warmer temperatures near the coast.\nOvernight lows will range from the low 40s inland just after the\nfrontal passage to the low 50s by the end of the week with\ncoastal locations in the upper 40s to low 50s early in the\nforecast period to low 50s to low 60s by the end of next week.\nWinds will remain breezy, particularly in the afternoons\ndepending upon the eventual track of the offshore nor'easter.",
